From: Stained Glass OS <ke7oxh@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] webservices: what Windows does in the XML writer, from Wine's todo_wine checks (fourteen removed)

Fourteen of the seventeen todo_wine checks in the writer tests pass now.

- Doubles are written as the shortest digits that read back as the same
  number, plain from 0.1 up to 1E+15 and with an exponent otherwise
  (0.1, 1E-2, 1.7976931348623157E+308). The old digit loop gave ":E-2" for
  0.01 on 64-bit.
- WsSetOutputToBuffer writes to a buffer the caller owns: the BYTES property
  is not there for it (E_INVALIDARG), and WsMoveWriter and WsGetWriterPosition
  need exactly such a buffer (WS_E_INVALID_OPERATION otherwise).
- WsWriteNode with no output set is WS_E_INVALID_OPERATION whatever the node.
- Conflicting namespace declarations make the element's end fail with
  WS_E_INVALID_FORMAT: one prefix declared for two namespaces, the element's
  own prefix declared for another one, an attribute in the xmlns prefix.
- Text of two different types cannot be written to one attribute
  (WS_E_INVALID_OPERATION); more of the same type adds to it.
